replaced by vertical lines without losing coverage: vertical lines cover all points in a column, while horizontal or slope -1 lines cover at most one point per column. So replacing them with vertical lines cannot reduce coverage. This is a valid argument.\n\nThen they reduce to covering T_k with k sunny lines. They derive bound on number of points a sunny line can cover in T_k using integer equation representation. They use p x - q y = c, with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. They derive parametric representation (x0+qt, y0+pt). Then sum x+y = (x0+y0)+(p+q)t. Since p+q ≠ 0 (sunny line not parallel to x+y=0), the sum changes by at least |p+q| per step. So number of integer points in T_k is at most floor((k+1-2)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). This is a valid counting argument. They also consider case |p+q|=1 separately, showing bound still holds. So bound is valid.\n\nThen they use counting argument: total points in T_k = k(k+1)/2. If each sunny line covers at most floor((k+1)/2) points, then k * floor((k+1)/2) >= total points needed. This yields condition that k must be odd. This is valid.\n\nThen they argue that for odd k, each line must achieve the bound exactly, and lines must be pairwise disjoint. This follows from equality in the inequality. If each line covers at most floor((k+1)/2) points, and total points needed equals k * floor((k+1)/2), then each line must cover exactly floor((k+1)/2) points, and there can be no overlap. This is valid.\n\nThen they claim that only three lines can achieve the bound, those with |p+q|=2, i.e., slopes 1, -2, -1/2. They argue that any other line would have |p+q|≥3 or |p+q|=1 with q≥2, leading to strictly smaller bound. This is valid reasoning: they derived bound (2.4) which is floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2) when |p+q|≥3? Let's check: For |p+q|≥3, floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≤ floor((k-1)/3)+1. For odd k, k=2m+1, (k-1)/3 = (2m)/3. floor((2m)/3)+1 ≤? For m≥1, floor((2m)/3)+1 ≤ m+1? Let's test m=1 (k=3): floor(2/3)+1 = 0