 not infinite. But points (a,b) for fixed a have varying b, so they are not collinear unless they lie on a line of slope 0 (horizontal) or slope -1? Let's examine: For a fixed a, points are (a,1), (a,2), ..., (a,n+1-a). These points are vertical line x=a. They are not collinear on any non-vertical line because they have same x coordinate but different y. So a non-vertical line cannot pass through more than one of them. So if we remove a vertical line x=a, we need to cover each point (a,b) individually with other lines. But we have only one sunny line to cover many points. It can cover at most one point from that column (since any non-vertical line can intersect the vertical line at most once). So we would need to cover the remaining points with other lines, but we have only n-1 vertical lines left, covering all columns except column a. So we need to cover the points in column a with the sunny line and maybe other lines? But we have no other lines left (since we have n-1 vertical lines covering all other columns). So the sunny line would need to cover all points in column a, which is impossible. So k=1 cannot be possible. Wait, maybe the solution uses a different arrangement: not all vertical lines, maybe some horizontal lines or slope -1 lines. Let's think more generally.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. Each line can cover at most n points? Let's think about maximum points a line can cover among these points. The points lie on integer lattice points in a triangular region. A line can pass through many of them if it aligns with some pattern. For example, the line x+y = constant passes through points with sum constant. Those are diagonal lines of slope -1. Those are not sunny (since they are parallel to x+y=0). So those are allowed as non-sunny lines. So we can use diagonal lines to cover points. Similarly, horizontal lines y = constant can cover points with same y coordinate. Those are also non-sunny. So we can use